From 35e2c8caa7e2701425d5a400f598ad279ccae00c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 05:12:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][VideoPlayer] A buffering of a slow source shows its
 progress and tells what the source sends

A laptop that played a 3D film of 3840x2160 at 48 frames a second over
Wi-Fi from a network share (SMB) waited 14.7 s for the first frame after
a seek of 30 minutes, with the picture standing still and nothing on it,
then played at about a third of its speed: "the input waits for its
source and the players ran dry". The demuxer had the stream 114 ms after
the seek; the rest was the source. Before the seek the same film had
played every frame for two minutes (2877 frames in 60 s).

While the player buffers (CACHESTATE_FULL) it plays on once what the
cache and its queues hold lasts longer than its queue time. The skin
shows that buffering with Player.CacheLevel, which was the read-ahead
level of the cache alone: the bytes ahead of the reader over the 1.5 GiB
that a cache of 2 GiB keeps ahead. While a slow source sends, the
demuxer takes every byte as it comes, so that level stays at 0, and
Estuary shows its ring only above 0.

- While the player buffers, Player.CacheLevel is how far it got to
  playing on (SOURCE_RATE::Progress(): the time held over the queue
  time, or the read-ahead level where that is higher). Out of a
  buffering it is the read-ahead level, as before.
- What the source sends is measured from where the input is and what
  the cache holds ahead of it, from the first pass of a buffering to its
  end. Once a buffering of 1 s or more ends, kodi.log says how long it
  took, what came, at what rate, and what the video takes on average
  (its length over its duration).
- Where the source has sent less than the video takes for 3 s of a
  buffering, the user is told once a file: "The source is slower than
  this video. It sends N Mbit/s; this video takes M Mbit/s. Playback
  stops now and then to wait for it." A source that stopped sending
  keeps its own notice after 5 s ("Connection interrupted").

Strings: core #62869 and #62870, Estuary #54928 and #54929, in English
and in the three Spanish translations of Estuary.
